n   193\t  (let [data (ex-data e)]\n   194\t    (if (contains? data :consumer)\n   195\t      (:consumer data)\n   196\t      handed)))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t(defn- carry-session\n   200\t  \"Rethrow `e`, keeping the throw and carrying the partial session in the\n   201\t   exception's data.\n   202\t\n   203\t   The original message and data are preserved and `:session` is added. The\n   204\t   rethrow is a new `ExceptionInfo` whose cause is the original, so a caller\n   205\t   matching the original exception's concrete type no longer matches the\n   206\t   wrapper — `(ex-cause caught)` names it. Recovery is opt-in: a caller that\n   207\t   does not read `:session` keeps the old semantics — a throw means the\n   208\t   round failed — and a caller that catches resumes from the carried\n   209\t   session, which names the furthest progress the round had made where the\n   210\t   failure happened.\"\n   211\t  [session e]\n   212\t  (throw (ex-info (or (ex-message e) (str e))\n   213\t                  (assoc (ex-data e) :session session)\n   214\t                  e)))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn run-on-stream\n   218\t  \"Coordinate one `{:observer observer :consumer consumer}` session over the\n   219\t   attached medium, returning the updated session.\n   220\t\n   221\t   The consumer is any value the three supplied functions agree on. `ready?`\n   222\t   decides when it may accept another batch, `load` hands it one observed\n   223\t   batch and returns the successor consumer, and `run` lets it do whatever\n   224\t   work a loaded batch implies — evaluate, expand, index, forward — and\n   225\t   returns the successor. This is coordination, not interpretation: no field\n   226\t   of the consumer is inspected, so any consumer shape can be driven.\n   227\t\n   228\t   A consumer that is not ready runs first, and observation follows only if\n   229\t   it becomes ready. A ready consumer observes one batch; `gap` commits the\n   230\t   recovery cursor and continues; `blocked` and `end` return the session.\n   231\t   After a batch loads and runs, a consumer that is ready again observes the\n   232\t   next batch, and one that is not returns the session without another read.\n   233\t\n   234\t   A `load` or `run` that throws still propagates — the throw is kept — but\n   235\t   carries the partial session in the exception's data under `:session`, so\n   236\t   recovery is opt-in. Where the failure happened decides the carried\n   237\t   session: a throwing `load` carries the cursor *before* the failing batch,\n   238\t   so a retry re-reads it; a throwing `run` carries the cursor *after* the\n   239\t   loaded batch and the consumer the failing `run` left — the partial state\n   240\t   it reported under `:consumer` in its own exception's data when it made\n   241\t   progress before throwing, else the state `load` returned — so a retry\n   242\t   neither re-reads the batch nor repeats work already delivered; a\n   243\t   terminal read after processed batches carries the session those batches\n   244\t   produced. A consumer whose input can be bad should therefore return that\n   245\t   as data from `load` rather than throw, and reserve the throw for its own\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
